        'Restaurant meals',
        'Package holidays',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The determinants line up: no substitutes, pure necessity, and immediate need. Note the contrast inside the same category: bottled water as a whole is fairly elastic (tap water is a substitute), but a specific brand is highly elastic (other brands are perfect substitutes) — market definition changes elasticity dramatically.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A government puts a £1 per unit tax on a good whose demand is highly inelastic and supply is elastic. Who mostly pays?',
      choices: [
        'The sellers, always',
        'Mostly the buyers — the side of the market that cannot walk away bears the tax: buyers barely cut quantity when the price rises, so sellers pass almost the whole £1 through',
        'Split exactly 50/50',
        'Nobody — the government absorbs it',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Tax incidence depends on relative elasticities, not on who legally remits the tax. With inelastic demand, quantity barely falls and the market price rises nearly the full tax. This is why fuel, tobacco and alcohol are such reliable tax bases — and why the same taxes are regressive, hitting poorer households harder.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Farmers have a bumper harvest, yet their total revenue falls. The explanation is:',
      choices: [
        'Buyers punish glut prices',
        'Food demand is inelastic: the larger crop forces prices down sharply while quantity rises only slightly — a 30% bigger harvest sold at 25% lower prices can leave revenue smaller, the classic "paradox of plenty"',
        'Supermarkets collude',
        'Quality falls with size',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'With inelastic demand, price and revenue move together: a big supply shift means a big price fall, and quantity barely compensates. It is also the flip side of farm-price supports: supply-side policies aimed at restricting output raise farm revenue precisely because demand for food is inelastic.',
    },
  ],
};

